<h2>The dispute</h2>
<p>The parties, married with children, have lived separately since February 2024, with a thicket of proceedings between them — a DV matter, a divorce case and a maintenance petition under Section 144 BNSS. The husband&rsquo;s suit before the Family Court, Patiala House, alleged that in November 2025 the wife came to his official residence at Altinho, Panaji, and levelled adultery allegations against him in front of staff, leading to a police complaint at PS Panjim. On 16 April 2026 the Family Court granted his interim application: no entry to the Goa residence-cum-office for the wife or her relatives, and no posting or publishing of &ldquo;incriminating material&rdquo; against him on WhatsApp, YouTube or Instagram pending the suit. The wife — who denies the allegations and asserts that the Goa house was a shared matrimonial home and that the husband is in an extra-marital relationship — appealed under Section 104 read with Order XLIII Rule 1(r) CPC.</p>

<h2>The four pillars of the affirmance</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>A reasonably possible view.</strong> The Family Court had considered prima facie case, balance of convenience and irreparable injury; the appellate court&rsquo;s role was not to re-weigh. <em>&ldquo;The appellate court is not required to substitute its own discretion.&rdquo;</em></div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>No roofless spouse.</strong> The wife resides in government accommodation at Rabindra Nagar, New Delhi, so the injunction created no risk of homelessness — the concern that drives shared-household jurisprudence.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The office in the residence.</strong> The premises double as the DGP&rsquo;s office; the husband <em>&ldquo;requires a conducive environment for the discharge of his official duties.&rdquo;</em></div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>No absolute right of entry.</strong> Marriage does not create <em>&ldquo;an absolute and indefeasible right&rdquo;</em> to enter particular premises, especially after two years of separation and with the matrimonial allegations untried.</div>
</div>

<h2>What the judgment does not decide</h2>
<p>The Court was careful about the boundary lines. The adultery allegations on both sides remain wholly untried and are for the Family Court. The clarification on <strong>Reena Grover v. Ramesh Grover</strong>, 2026 SCC OnLine Del 512, matters for DV practice: the existence of alternative accommodation is a weighty factor, not an automatic disqualification from access to a shared household. And the social-media restraint was affirmed as an interim measure in aid of a fair trial, not as a finding that any past post was defamatory.</p>

